14.	What is a Dynamic block in TF?

In Terraform (TF), a dynamic block is a way to generate nested configuration blocks dynamically based on variable data — usually when you need to repeat a block multiple times but don’t know the exact count or structure until runtime.

This is especially useful when you work with resources that have nested arguments (like security_rule inside an azurerm_network_security_group) and you want to avoid hardcoding them.

Why it exists
Normally in Terraform, if a resource has a nested block, you must write it explicitly:

hcl
Copy
Edit
resource "azurerm_network_security_group" "example" {
  name                = "example-nsg"
  location            = azurerm_resource_group.example.location
  resource_group_name = azurerm_resource_group.example.name

  security_rule {
    name                       = "AllowSSH"
    priority                   = 100
    direction                  = "Inbound"
    access                     = "Allow"
    protocol                   = "Tcp"
    source_port_range          = "*"
    destination_port_range     = "22"
    source_address_prefix      = "*"
    destination_address_prefix = "*"
  }
}
If you have many rules, that’s repetitive. A dynamic block lets you loop through a list or map and generate those blocks programmatically.

How it works
Syntax:

hcl
Copy
Edit
dynamic "<BLOCK_TYPE>" {
  for_each = <ITERABLE>
  content {
    # properties of each block
  }
}
Example:

hcl
Copy
Edit
variable "rules" {
  default = [
    {
      name     = "AllowSSH"
      priority = 100
      port     = "22"
    },
    {
      name     = "AllowHTTP"
      priority = 101
      port     = "80"
    }
  ]
}

resource "azurerm_network_security_group" "example" {
  name                = "example-nsg"
  location            = azurerm_resource_group.example.location
  resource_group_name = azurerm_resource_group.example.name

  dynamic "security_rule" {
    for_each = var.rules
    content {
      name                       = security_rule.value.name
      priority                   = security_rule.value.priority
      direction                  = "Inbound"
      access                     = "Allow"
      protocol                   = "Tcp"
      source_port_range          = "*"
      destination_port_range     = security_rule.value.port
      source_address_prefix      = "*"
      destination_address_prefix = "*"
    }
  }
}

Key points for an interview
Purpose → Automates creation of repeated nested blocks based on data.
When to use → When the number/structure of nested blocks is variable or large.
Syntax → Uses dynamic "<block_type>" { for_each = ... content { ... } }.
Scope of iteration → Inside content, access each element as <block_type>.value.
Limitation → Only works for nested blocks, not top-level resource arguments.

If you’re explaining it for an Azure DevOps + Terraform interview, you can say:
"In Terraform, a dynamic block is used to dynamically generate nested configuration blocks based on variable input.
For example, in Azure, when defining multiple NSG rules in an azurerm_network_security_group, instead of writing each rule manually, I can loop over a list of rules using a dynamic block.
This makes infrastructure code cleaner, more maintainable, and adaptable to different environments without hardcoding."
