19.	How to connect to Azure for terraform without storing any kind of credentials on the machine?

If they’re asking this in an Azure DevOps interview, they’re testing whether you know how to authenticate Terraform against Azure without saving secrets on the local machine — meaning no az login with cached credentials, no service principal files, and no ARM_CLIENT_SECRET sitting in plain text.

Here’s how you can answer:

1. Use Managed Identity (best for no credentials at all)
If Terraform runs inside an Azure resource (Azure DevOps pipeline agent, Azure VM, Azure Container Instance, AKS, etc.) that has a Managed Identity assigned, Azure will automatically issue an access token without needing stored credentials.
In Terraform, you just enable Azure CLI authentication or set:
bash
Copy
Edit
export ARM_USE_MSI=true
or in provider config:
hcl
Copy
Edit
provider "azurerm" {
  features {}
  use_msi = true
}
Azure automatically handles the token rotation.

2. Use Azure DevOps Service Connection (with OIDC)
In Azure DevOps Pipelines, you can use Federated Credentials (OIDC) to let the pipeline request a token from Azure AD.
Steps:
Create a Service Connection of type Azure Resource Manager with Workload Identity Federation (no secrets).
Link it to a Service Principal in Azure AD with Federated credentials tied to your pipeline.
Terraform runs with ARM_USE_OIDC or via the Azure DevOps Terraform task, which pulls a short-lived token at runtime.
This way:
No client_secret
No credential files
All authentication is via short-lived tokens.

3. Use Azure CLI Logged-in Session in Cloud Shell
If Terraform is executed inside Azure Cloud Shell, it already has an authenticated Azure CLI context.
The provider uses this automatically:
hcl
Copy
Edit
provider "azurerm" {
  features {}
}

No credentials are stored locally — the shell’s ephemeral environment handles authentication.
✅ Key points to say in the interview:
Use Managed Identity or OIDC Federated Identity to completely avoid static secrets.
Rely on short-lived tokens from Azure AD at runtime.
No need to store credentials on the machine — either run in a trusted Azure-hosted environment or use pipeline service connections.

