2) Infrastructure-as-Code using Terraform — approach and module structure

Principles:
Single source of truth for infra.
Small, focused, reusable modules.
Remote state backend with locking (Azure Storage + blob lease).
Use workspaces or separate state files per environment (dev/stage/prod).
Keep secrets out of TF state (use Key Vault, pass secrets at runtime via pipeline).
CI/CD runs terraform plan & apply with service principal & least-privilege.
Suggested repo structure:

infra/
├── README.md
├── environments/
│   ├── dev/
│   │   └── main.tf (calls modules, backend config)
│   ├── stage/
│   └── prod/
└── modules/
    ├── network/
    │   ├── main.tf
    │   ├── variables.tf
    │   └── outputs.tf
    ├── identity/
    ├── storage_account/
    ├── keyvault/
    ├── aks/
    ├── acr/
    ├── aml_workspace/
    └── monitoring/


Module design guidelines:
Single responsibility per module: network only for VNets/subnets; aks only for cluster; keyvault only secrets & policies.
Inputs: explicit variables for all configurable items (CIDR ranges, env name, SKU, tags).
Outputs: only necessary attributes for wiring modules (vnet_id, subnet_ids, aks_cluster_name).
Versioning: publish modules (if shared) with semantic versions.
Example: modules/network/variables.tf

variable "env" { type = string }
variable "location" { type = string }
variable "vnet_address_space" { type = string }
variable "subnets" {
  type = map(object({ cidr = string, service_endpoints = optional(list(string)) }))
}

modules/network/main.tf creates the VNet, subnets, NSGs, and optionally route tables. Use for_each to create subnets from a map.
Environment environments/prod/main.tf minimal:
provider "azurerm" { features = {} }
module "network" {
  source = "../../modules/network"
  env = "prod"
  location = var.location
  vnet_address_space = "10.0.0.0/16"
  subnets = {
    infra = { cidr = "10.0.1.0/24" }
    aks   = { cidr = "10.0.2.0/24" }
    storage = { cidr = "10.0.3.0/24" }
  }
}

State & backend:
Use Azure Storage Account with a dedicated container for tfstate.
Enable blob versioning and store state backups regularly.
Enable state locking implicitly through blob leasing via azurerm backend.

Workflows:
Pull request CI runs terraform plan with -var-file=env.tfvars.
Merge triggers terraform apply using a service principal with only necessary permissions.

