3) AKS vs Azure App Service vs ACI for ML workloads — when to use each

Short summary then details.
Azure Kubernetes Service (AKS)
Best for: scalable, production-grade inference & batch workloads; GPU-based inference; complex microservices; can run multi-container pods and autoscale precisely.
Pros:
Fine-grained control over compute, storage, networking.
GPU node pools for GPU inference/training.
HPA / VPA and cluster autoscaler → granular scaling.
Integrates with KNative/KServe, Istio, Prometheus, DVC tools.
Cons:
Operational overhead: cluster upgrades, node pool management.
Use cases: heavy inference traffic, custom autoscaling rules, multi-component GenAI stacks (retrieve + rerank + LM).
Azure App Service
Best for: simple model web-APIs with light CPU usage; quick container hosting without managing k8s.
Pros:
PaaS — minimal infra management.
TLS, autoscaling, staging slots built-in.
Cons:
No GPU support (so not suitable for heavy ML/GenAI).
Less control over networking & complex routing.
Use cases: lightweight ML microservices, small REST wrappers around small models or ensemble predictions when latency/throughput is modest.
Azure Container Instances (ACI)
Best for: ephemeral containers, ad-hoc inference, batch jobs, dev/test.
Pros:
Fast spin-up, no cluster management.
Cost-effective for low-duration runs.
Cons:
Not for high throughput or production service-level traffic.
Limited autoscaling; not integrated with K8s HPA (but can integrate via virtual kubelet).
Use cases: spot-check predictions, playgrounds, short-lived batch inference, fallback for AKS spike via virtual-kubelet.
Decision matrix summary
Production, GPU, high-throughput → AKS (or Azure ML managed endpoints backed by AKS).
Quick, low-ops, CPU-only small services → App Service.
Short-lived tasks, dev/test, low scale → ACI.

