5) Terraform deployment failure — recovery & reconcile state (practical playbook)

When a terraform apply fails mid-way, state can be inconsistent between real infra and the tfstate file. Steps to recover safely:
Prevention (best practice up front)
Use remote backend with locks (azurerm backend with blob locking).
Enable state versioning & backups in Storage Account.
Make small, atomic changes in PRs.
Use CI gating: run terraform plan in PR and apply only on merge with pipeline.
Recovery steps (reactive)
Don’t panic. Stop further applies to that backend (others might be blocked by state lock).
Inspect logs & error message from the failed terraform apply — identify which resource failed and which ones were created.
Query real cloud state:
Use Azure Portal / az cli to list resources created in the failed apply.
az resource list --tag terraform or check resource group.
Run terraform state list to see what state Terraform believes exists.
Run terraform refresh to update local state from real infra (this compares resources and updates attributes). Note: terraform refresh modifies state — use with care.
terraform init then terraform refresh -var-file=...
If refresh cannot find resources that exist (or finds inconsistencies), use terraform import to import the real resource into state:
terraform import module.aks.azurerm_kubernetes_cluster.aks <resource-id>
After import, run terraform plan to see differences.
If a resource was partially created and cannot be imported cleanly, consider:
terraform state rm to remove a dangling state entry (if state claims a resource exists but it doesn't) — then recreate with apply.
terraform state mv to rename/move resources in state (rare).
Manual state editing only as last resort. If you must, download state file, edit carefully, and re-upload. Prefer terraform state commands over manual editing.
Re-run terraform plan and validate the plan only contains intended changes.
Apply once plan is clean.
If state file is corrupted, restore from Storage Account blob version / backup and re-run terraform plan. Keep a copy of the corrupted state for audit.
Useful commands summary
terraform init
terraform plan -var-file=env.tfvars
terraform apply -var-file=env.tfvars  # failed
# Inspect:
terraform state list
terraform refresh
# To import a resource that exists:
terraform import module.module_name.resource_type.resource_name <resource-id>
# To remove a bad state entry:
terraform state rm <address>
# To move state between addresses:
terraform state mv <from> <to>
Example scenarios & remediation
Scenario: AKS created but nodepool not fully ready
Inspect AKS via az aks show and az aks nodepool list. Import any resources missing in state and then re-apply.
Scenario: Storage account created but TF state missing
terraform import azurerm_storage_account.main /subscriptions/.../resourceGroups/.../providers/Microsoft.Storage/storageAccounts/myacct
Scenario: Backend locked by dead process
Investigate lock (blob lease). With caution, you can break the lock (Azure storage tools) only if you know no other apply is running.
Automation & safety additions
Use a pre-apply drift detection job: run terraform plan daily to detect drift.
Keep tfstate snapshots and automated backups (Storage blob versioning).
Build a reconciliation script that uses Azure APIs to report drift between infra and state.
